.\" uping(8) — manual page for the Debian package.
.TH UPING 8 "2026-11-22" "uping 0.4.0" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
uping \- TOML-configured reverse proxy with automatic HTTPS, static files and raw TCP forwarding
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uping
.RI [ CONFIG ]
.RB [ \-\-check | \-\-dry\-run | \-\-dns\-zone ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B uping
terminates TLS for one or more domains and forwards each of them to a local
backend or a directory of files. One HTTPS listener answers every configured
name by SNI, and plain HTTP is redirected to it; a domain can also get its own
listener with
.IR https_listen .
.PP
Certificates come from one of three places, chosen per domain with
.IR keys :
public ACME such as Let's Encrypt
.RB ( auto ),
uping's own certificate authority
.RB ( internal ),
which generates a root on first start, signs only
.I [internal] domain
and its subdomains with no network access at all, and can publish that root at
the
.I [internal] serve_root
URL, or two PEM files you provide
.RB ( manual ).
.PP
A
.I [[server]]
entry may proxy to an upstream, serve a directory, or split the path with
.IR routes ;
per-IP request and connection limits, upstream health checks and an
raw TCP forwarder
.RB ( [[stream]] )
are also available.
.PP
The configuration is read once at startup and again on
.BR SIGHUP .
Relative paths in it resolve against the directory that holds the file.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage line and exit.
.TP
.BR \-\-check ", " \-\-dry\-run
Validate
.I CONFIG
the way a start would, and exit. Nothing is bound, nothing is written and no
certificate is requested: a domain whose certificate is still missing is
reported as pending, not as an error. Exit status 0 means the file would load.
.TP
.B \-\-dns\-zone
Print
.BR hosts (5)
records, one line per configured domain and address family, for the private
resolver of a VPN setup. A wildcard listen address is refused, because a record
must name a real address. Nothing is written.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/uping/config.toml
The system configuration. The package installs a loopback-only default when the
file does not exist and never overwrites it afterwards.
.TP
.I /var/lib/uping
Writable state owned by the
.I uping
user: the local CA and the generated leaves.
.TP
.I /usr/share/uping/config.toml.example
A commented template with the public, private and static shapes.
.TP
.I /usr/share/doc/uping/
The user manual
.RI ( README.md )
and the package notes
.RI ( README.Debian ).
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGHUP
Re-read the configuration and apply what can change without rebinding: domains,
upstreams, static roots, routes, limits, health settings, certificates and the
domains each listener answers. An invalid file is logged and the running
configuration is kept. Changes to
.IR [listen] ,
.IR https_listen ,
.IR [[stream]] ,
.I [acme]
or
.I [internal]
need a restart.
.TP
.B SIGTERM ", " SIGINT
Stop serving and exit.
.SH SERVICE
The package ships a system unit that runs as the unprivileged
.I uping
user with an ambient
.B CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE
capability:
.PP
.RS 4
systemctl enable \-\-now uping
.br
systemctl reload uping
.br
systemctl restart uping
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Normal shutdown, or a successful
.B \-\-check
or
.B \-\-dns\-zone
run.
.TP
.B 1
The configuration could not be read, parsed or validated, or the service could
not start.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd.service (5),
.BR hosts (5)
.PP
The full configuration reference is the README shipped in
.IR /usr/share/doc/uping/ .
